artifact.self_contained_problem:
Determine whether the hadronisation of heavy quarks (charm and beauty) is a universal process across different collision systems (e+e−, ep, pp, p–A, and A–A), or whether it instead exhibits system-dependent features that require collision-system-specific mechanisms such as: (i) hadronisation via quark coalescence/recombination in a dense partonic medium; (ii) the contribution of so-far-unobserved higher-mass excited charm-baryon states feeding down to ground-state baryons; or (iii) string formation beyond the leading-colour approximation (e.g. colour-reconnection or junction mechanisms).

Concretely, identify a set of observables and a quantitative framework that discriminate between these competing hadronisation pictures. The observables of interest include the production cross sections, transverse-momentum spectra, baryon-to-meson ratios, and fragmentation fractions of the following heavy-flavour hadrons measured across collision systems:
 • single-charm and single-beauty baryons: Λc+, Λb0, Ξc+, Ξc0, Ωc0;
 • the doubly-heavy meson Bc+ (containing b and c quarks);
 • multi-charm baryons: Ξcc+, Ξcc++, Ωcc+, Ωccc++;
 • exotic heavy-flavour hadrons such as X(3872) and Tcc+.

The problem is to:
(a) formulate criteria, in terms of these observables, under which the hypothesis of universal heavy-quark hadronisation is falsified;
(b) determine which combinations of measurements among the hadrons listed above are sufficient to distinguish between coalescence-based models, models invoking an augmented spectrum of higher-mass charm baryons, and models with non-leading-colour string formation; and
(c) characterise how the production of exotic states such as X(3872) and Tcc+ depends on the hadronisation mechanism and the collision environment.

